---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="lbs-to-kg">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="lbs-to-kg" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Multiply by 0.45359237</h2>
    <p>
      To convert lbs to kg, multiply by the definition: one avoirdupois pound is exactly 0.45359237 kg.
      Nobody measured that number. Six countries agreed it in 1959, and it has not changed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The mental shortcut is to halve: 150 lb is about 75 kg. That is 10% high. Halve, then subtract a
      tenth: 75 minus 7.5 is 67.5, against a true 68.04. Close enough for a suitcase.
    </p>

    <h2>Entering stone and pounds</h2>
    <p>
      British weights are two numbers. Type <code>11 st 4 lb</code> and the page reads 158 lb and
      converts it to 71.67 kg. You can also type plain pounds or decimal stone, and the page shows which
      reading it used. The stone-and-pounds readout below the result repeats your entry, so you can see
      a transposed digit at once.
    </p>

    <h2>Reference points</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Pounds</th><th scope="col">Kilograms</th><th scope="col">Stone and pounds</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>100</td><td>45.36</td><td>7 st 2 lb</td></tr>
        <tr><td>120</td><td>54.43</td><td>8 st 8 lb</td></tr>
        <tr><td>140</td><td>63.50</td><td>10 st 0 lb</td></tr>
        <tr><td>150</td><td>68.04</td><td>10 st 10 lb</td></tr>
        <tr><td>160</td><td>72.57</td><td>11 st 6 lb</td></tr>
        <tr><td>180</td><td>81.65</td><td>12 st 12 lb</td></tr>
        <tr><td>200</td><td>90.72</td><td>14 st 4 lb</td></tr>
        <tr><td>250</td><td>113.40</td><td>17 st 12 lb</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Medicine runs on kilograms</h2>
    <p>
      Paediatric and anaesthetic dosing is per kilogram, so hospitals record weight in kilograms, also in
      countries that use pounds. A pound figure converted and rounded before dosing is a known source of
      error. The standard fix is to weigh in kilograms directly. For a medical form, use the unconverted
      measurement where you can.
    </p>

    <h2>Baggage, shipping and the tonne</h2>
    <p>
      A 50 lb US baggage limit is 22.68 kg, slightly under the 23 kg used almost everywhere else. For
      freight, a metric tonne is 1,000 kg (2,204.6 lb), a US short ton is 2,000 lb (907.18 kg) and a UK
      long ton is 2,240 lb (1,016.05 kg). The three units differ by up to 12%, so always check which ton
      a quote means.
    </p>

    <h2>Not the troy pound</h2>
    <p>
      Precious metals use troy weight, where a pound is 12 troy ounces and 373.24 g, not 453.59 g. If
      your figure came from a bullion dealer, this page is the wrong tool.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
